# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!services/universe-service/.env.local
!services/nature-service/.env.local
!services/dna-service/.env.local
!services/api-gateway/.env.local
!apps/myunivokai-web/.env.local
!apps/myunivokai-admin/.env.local
!infra/.env.local
!services/auth-service/.env.local
!services/analytics-service/.env.local
!services/telemetry-service/.env.local
!.env.local

# Node / Next.js
node_modules/
.next/
dist/

# Go build artifacts
bin/

# Rust build artifacts. One `target/` per crate — contracts/rust and
# services/telemetry-service are separate crates, the same way each Go service
# has its own go.mod.
target/

# A library crate's lock file is ignored and a binary's is committed, which is
# Cargo's own convention: the lock only takes effect for the thing being built,
# so contracts/rust's would never be read by anyone, while
# services/telemetry-service's is what makes a Render build resolve the exact
# versions CI tested.
contracts/rust/Cargo.lock

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# TypeScript incremental build cache
*.tsbuildinfo

.vscode

# Playwright's own run artefacts: traces, videos, retries, the HTML report.
# Large, per-run, and reproducible by re-running the suite.
apps/myunivokai-web/playwright-report/
apps/myunivokai-web/test-results/

# e2e/shots/ is deliberately NOT ignored.
#
# It used to be, with the reasoning that WebGL output is GPU-dependent and the
# images only matter for one afternoon. The first half is wrong: playwright.config
# forces --use-angle=swiftshader, so every frame is a SOFTWARE render and two
# machines running the same commit produce the same pixels. That is what makes
# them worth keeping.
#
# The second half is wrong too. This repository's scene work has produced a run
# of bugs that only an image could show — water rendering grey, a frame clipped
# flat to white, fish above the waterline, a street lamp on the seabed — and in
# every case the question asked afterwards was "when did this start". Committed
# frames answer it; a local folder that is wiped on every checkout does not.

# Demo builds: three.js is spliced in at build time, never committed.
demos/*/dist/
